WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.142
This query calculates a simple completeness score.

00:00:03.142 --> 00:00:08.137
Then ROW_NUMBER ranks rows inside each normalized duplicate group.

00:00:08.137 --> 00:00:14.707
The most complete row ranks first, with signup date and ID used as additional ordering.

00:00:14.707 --> 00:00:20.597
This does not mean the first row is always truly correct.

00:00:20.597 --> 00:00:23.122
It means the rule is explicit, reproducible, and reviewable.

00:00:23.122 --> 00:00:28.697
Duplicate resolution requires a definition of identity and a survivor rule.

00:00:28.697 --> 00:00:34.427
Exact duplicate rows are easy; duplicate people or customers are harder.

00:00:34.427 --> 00:00:42.372
If you choose one survivor, document the priority—latest update, verified source, highest completeness,

00:00:42.372 --> 00:00:46.880
or another defensible rule—and preserve links to the merged records.
